 ## Overview
-This method implements GPU-accelerated direct volume rendering specifically for interactive light field displays using Multi-Camera Optical Projection (MCOP) [S1]. It solves the problem of generating correct per-pixel ray parameters for a multi-projector display where standard fixed-function pipeline interpolation fails, producing curved lines and incorrect ray origins [S1]. Unlike traditional multiview technologies that restrict users to discrete viewing zones, this approach provides a fully continuous perceived image by computing rays from projector positions through screen pixels into the volume [S1]. It distinguishes itself by using a coarsely tessellated proxy geometry to compute MCOP projections in the vertex shader and employing depth-dependent mipmap level selection to match spatial resolution to the display's angular spread [S1].
+This method accelerates direct volume rendering on an interactive light field display by computing rays from projector positions through screen pixels into the volume using Multi-Camera Optical Projection (MCOP) [S1]. It solves the problem of incorrect per-pixel ray parameters caused by standard vertex attribute interpolation in non-linear projections, ensuring a fully continuous perceived image for multiple viewers [S1]. Unlike traditional multiview technologies that restrict users to fixed positions due to discrete viewing zones, this approach provides continuous parallax and accommodation cues [S1].
 
 ## How it works
+The method implements a GPU-based ray casting pipeline where ray parameters are computed via MCOP projection in the vertex shader, and volume integration occurs in the fragment shader with depth-dependent mipmap selection [S1].
 
